# Saved data and backups

- A server's data directory is a named volume the daemon creates (`docker.NewDataVolumeName`, labelled, verified after create) and records on the row (`data_volume`); `start` creates it when the row has none or the recorded one is gone (job log line, fingerprint forgotten) and mounts it at `build.DataDir`. A restore or reset replaces it with a new name. `Delete` removes container, then volume, then row. `RemoveVolume` refuses the current volume of any row and volumes in `pendingVolumes` (created by a running job, not yet recorded).
- Everything read from a volume is untrusted. `backups.Normalize` keeps only regular files and directories with clean relative paths (`cleanRel`: UTF-8, no controls, no `.`/`..`, bounded length and depth), each inside a kept directory, zeroes uid/gid, masks modes, drops PAX records, enforces `--backup-max-mb` and `MaxEntries` while streaming, and reads only the top-level `world.snapshot` header (`ParseSnapshotHeader`, strict; "" when unknown). Skipped names reach logs only through `logsafe`. `Rewrite` re-checks a stored archive entry by entry against the recorded counts before `docker cp` in, with uid/gid 65532.
- Archives: `backups.Store` writes `.tmp-*`, syncs, renames to `<32 hex>.tar.gz`, and only then the caller inserts the row; rows are deleted before files; `Sweep` (startup, before jobs) removes temp files and archives without a row. `Acquire` marks a backup read by a running restore; `Delete` refuses it (`ErrInUse` → 409). Free space keeps `staticfiles.DefaultReserveBytes`.
- Helpers: every volume read or write goes through `createHelper` (verified build image, `docker.HelperSpec`), never started, removed in a deferred `removeHelper` (also on failure and cancellation). The launch id is recorded (`volume_helpers`, synced) before the create and the container id after; `CleanupHelpers` (startup and every schedule tick) removes only recorded helpers not in `activeHelpers`; `knownHelpers` keeps them out of foreign-container logging. Never act on a helper found by labels alone.
- Jobs: `Backup` (`server.backup`), `Restore` (`server.restore`, admin, restricted) and `ResetData` (`server.data_reset`, admin, restricted) run under the server subject. A live backup calls `gameadmin.Client.Snapshot` (409 waits `SnapshotRetry` and retries, 501 or no answer copies as is, other failures fail). Restore: `checkRestore` in the handler (`CheckRestore`) and again in the job; stop without changing desired state; safety backup (`pre_restore`/`pre_reset`, skipped without data); fresh volume plus helper copy; then one `SwitchServerData` write; then old container and volume removal (failure logged); start through `start` unless a forced mismatch. Any failure before the switch leaves the old volume recorded and starts the server again when it is meant to run.
- Schema guard: `schemaMismatch(data, build)` is true only when both are known and differ. `start` checks it right after reading the build and the volume state, before touching any container. `ready` records the game's fingerprint on the build and on the row (`SetServerDataFingerprint`, only for the volume it started with). Builds read the committed fingerprint at build time (see the builds rule).
- Schedule: `ScheduleBackups` (daemon loop every `ScheduleInterval`) starts `scheduledBackup` jobs for running servers whose newest backup is older than the interval, skipping servers whose last scheduled backup failed within `ScheduleRetryDelay`; retention deletes only `scheduled` backups beyond `keep`.
- Handlers decode and check (note, `backup_id`, server exists, no active job) and map errors in `serversError` (`ErrBackupNotFound` 404, `ErrBackupInUse`/`ErrBackupDamaged`/`ErrNoBuild`/`ErrVolumeCurrent` 409 conflict, `*SchemaMismatchError` 409 `schema_mismatch`). Downloads use `http.ServeContent` with an attachment name built from `[a-z0-9-]` only.
- Verify with `go test ./internal/backups/... ./internal/servers/... ./internal/store/... ./internal/httpapi/... ./internal/docker/... ./internal/gameadmin/...`; with Docker `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/docker/... ./internal/httpapi/...` (`TestIntegrationDataVolumes`, `TestIntegrationBackupsAPI` with the fake game's snapshot endpoint).
